  Check the section on wizardly advice here:
<http://www.mindspring.com/~jove/Guides.htm>

  Find ttyrec of mrivan's Game 4 wizard ascension.
<http://www.mindspring.com/~jove/mrivan_nao_ttyrec_index.htm>

  Wizards are death on the hoof early on.  Their own death.  Basically
use every trick you can find.  Use pets, try on non-cursed armor for
enchantment, throw daggers, Elbereth, bravely run away!.

  Armor and spellcasting gets incredibly intricate.

  Magic missile is the wizard special spell.  Even with metallic body
armor the failure rate is only 4% with Int:18 at XL:1.  If no other
metal armor is worn.

  Here's a webpage that lets you see spell failure rates at different
XL, Int, skill level, and armor types.  Try it to see various
combinations.
<http://www.gridbug.de/spells.html> 

  Metallic shoes and helm give only a 4% failure rate for force bolt
even at XL:1.  One +3 iron helmet and you're in great shape.


  Metal armor and spellcasting aren't an either-or situation.  Not all
metal armor hurts spell casting the same.  Armor can be taken off for
spell casting as well.  Even the most power-mad wizard spends less
than 1% of turns casting spells.

  Most spells can be cast off-line (i.e. when not in melee combat.)
Light, knock, healing, haste self, identify, etc. all can be useful
when cast from a safe place.

  Shields are a prime example.  A highly enchanted shield can make a
huge difference in defense.  It will also cut spell success rates by
75%.  Yet it's just one turn to remove and another turn to wear again.
You can cast force bolt to soften up an approaching enemy, then wear
the shield as it reaches melee range.  A +4 elven shield (which I have
found) lowers your AC by 6, and is light.  Just remember to wear it
when not casting spells.

 
  You'll find lots of helms, boots, and shields.  Try on the
non-cursed ones for enchantment.  Try on mithril as well.  If you find
+4 elven mithril, wear it.  I have.  That may mean locking yourself in
a closet to safely remove the armor for spellcasting.  It's probably
worth it.

  Leather golems are a great source of leather armor to try.  I've
found +4 before.  (+3 leather armor is the equivalent of +0 elven
mithril.)


